// ============================================================================
// Records digitisation: platform capability assessment. One row per business
// platform, answering ONE question.
// Render the ERD by pasting this file into https://dbdiagram.io
//
// THE QUESTION IS NOT "can this platform store documents". It is: can it KEEP
// A RECORD, for a retention period that in a health service reaches decades.
// A disposal standard gives that test exactly two lawful shapes. A system is
// acceptable when it either manages retention and disposal IN PLACE, or lets
// the records and their required metadata be EXTRACTED into another system
// that will then manage them. Every column here earns its place by helping
// answer one of those two, and DestinationVerdict is where the answer lands.
//
// SCOPED TO THE HEADLINE VERDICT, DELIBERATELY. An earlier design ran to
// forty-four columns and a second to sixty-seven, each argued from a standard,
// and both were right about the facts and wrong about the artefact: a form
// nobody finishes produces no verdict at all. What survives is platform
// identity, six capability answers, three lists of evidence, the verdict, and
// who said so. Everything else belongs in the digitisation plan document,
// which is a document, and this is a register.
//
// NO YES/NO COLUMN EXCEPT ONE. A checkbox cannot say UNKNOWN, and most of this
// list is filled in by a platform custodian BEFORE the assessment interview,
// where "I do not know" is the most common honest answer. An unticked box and
// a considered No render identically. So the six capability questions share
// one capability_answer enum with Unknown first and defaulted, and the single
// surviving boolean is FollowUpRequired, which the assessor owns and therefore
// always knows.
//
// THE VERDICT IS TYPED BY A PERSON, not calculated. A calculated column is
// read-only, so there would be nowhere to record "the answers say no, we
// accept the risk with named configuration, and here is why" - which is the
// assessor's actual job. It also could not be indexed, and every operational
// view here filters on it.
//
// THREE COLUMNS HOLD MANY VALUES, and this is the first shipped family to use
// the enum_name[] form. Which disposal-suspension triggers a platform honours,
// which audit events it logs and which export routes it offers are genuinely
// lists, and flattening them into prose destroys the cross-platform comparison
// the register exists to produce. What that costs is real and is stated in
// 20-configure/mapping.yaml beside every place it bites: no index, no default,
// no unique, no colour, no formula may read one, and a view may only ask
// includes / not_includes / is_null / is_not_null.
//
// NO COMMERCIAL PRODUCT IS NAMED, in the demo rows or anywhere else. A shipped
// row saying "no bulk export, verdict: not a destination" beside a vendor name
// would be a fabricated capability claim about somebody else's product,
// pasted into every adopter tenant. The demo rows name generic roles.
//
// NO RECORD CONTENT, at any grain. This register holds platform metadata. The
// note columns take CATEGORIES, never examples, because an example of a record
// title in a health service is routinely a patient name. See
// 50-govern/governance.md.
// ============================================================================

Project records_digitisation { database_type: 'SharePoint Online Lists' }

// THE ANSWER SET, shared by all six capability questions.
//
// Unknown is FIRST and is the default, so an untouched row reads as unanswered
// rather than as answered No. Partly is the answer this domain actually
// produces: versions the documents but not the attachments; logs the events
// but does not retain the log.
Enum capability_answer {
  "Unknown"
  "No"
  "Partly"
  "Yes"
}

// Where the platform sits in the organisation. Edit these to the way your
// service divides work; nothing in the build depends on the wording.
Enum business_domain {
  "Clinical"
  "Quality and safety"
  "People and culture"
  "Finance and procurement"
  "Corporate and executive"
  "Supporting systems"
}

// A platform being decommissioned is not a destination for anything, whatever
// else it scores, and a platform still being implemented cannot have been
// assessed against what it will finally do. The default view filters Retired
// away, which is how the register stays usable as it accumulates.
Enum platform_lifecycle {
  "Being implemented"
  "In service"
  "Under review"
  "Decommissioning"
  "Retired"
}

// THE HEADLINE VERDICT, built on the two lawful shapes rather than on a
// score. "Manages retention and disposal in place" is the first shape.
// "Interim only - export with metadata proven" is the second, and it says
// interim because a platform that can only hand the records on is a staging
// point rather than a home. "Suitable with named configuration" is the
// answer that makes this register worth keeping: the platform can do it, and
// somebody has to write down what has to be turned on first.
Enum destination_verdict {
  "Not assessed"
  "Manages retention and disposal in place"
  "Suitable with named configuration"
  "Interim only - export with metadata proven"
  "Not a destination"
}

// MULTI-VALUE. Disposal has to be suspendable on several independent grounds
// and a platform commonly honours some and not others, so a single Yes/No
// would average four different answers into one. "Legal hold" is deliberately
// not the wording: it is narrower than the set.
Enum disposal_suspension_trigger {
  "Legal proceedings"
  "Unfinalised access request"
  "Unfinalised audit or investigation"
  "Organisational freeze"
}

// MULTI-VALUE. "The platform has an audit log" is not a fact anybody can act
// on. Which events reach it is, and Export and Delete are the two that decide
// whether a disposal action can ever be evidenced.
Enum audit_event {
  "View"
  "Create"
  "Edit"
  "Export"
  "Delete"
  "Permission change"
}

// MULTI-VALUE. A platform frequently has more than one route out and they are
// not equivalent: a vendor-run extract is a purchase order and a lead time,
// self-service is a Tuesday afternoon, and "one item at a time" is not an
// export route at all for a collection of any size. An empty set is the
// finding, and the "No bulk export route" view is built on it.
Enum export_method {
  "Vendor-run bulk extract"
  "Self-service bulk export"
  "Documented API extract"
  "Report output only"
  "One item at a time"
}

Table Platform {
  Id                          int                          [pk, increment]
  Title                       nvarchar                     [not null, note: 'What the platform is called here, as staff would say it. A generic name, not a vendor product name']
  BusinessDomain              business_domain              [not null, note: 'Which part of the organisation this platform serves. It decides who has to be in the room for the assessment']
  PlatformCustodian           person                       [note: 'The person here who answers for this platform day to day. They fill the six capability answers in before the assessment interview']
  LifecycleStatus             platform_lifecycle           [not null, default: 'In service', note: 'What the platform itself is doing. A platform in Decommissioning is not a destination for anything, whatever else it scores']

  // --- Can it keep a record ------------------------------------------------
  // Six questions, one shared answer set, in the order an assessor asks them.
  // Each one is a capability the disposal standard requires of a system that
  // is going to hold records to their full retention.
  RetentionAndDisposalInPlace capability_answer            [not null, default: 'Unknown', note: 'Can retention periods be applied in the platform itself, and can it dispose of records when they fall due? This is the first of the two lawful shapes']
  DisposalIsEvidenced         capability_answer            [not null, default: 'Unknown', note: 'When something is disposed of, does the platform leave a record of what went, when, and under what authority? Disposal that cannot be evidenced cannot be defended']
  DestructionIsComplete       capability_answer            [not null, default: 'Unknown', note: 'Does deletion actually destroy, including copies in backups, preservation stores and disaster recovery? Soft delete over seven years of backups is the commonest way a platform looks compliant and is not']
  DisposalCanBeSuspended      capability_answer            [not null, default: 'Unknown', note: 'Can disposal be frozen over a defined set of records while a matter is open, and lifted afterwards? Record which grounds it honours in Suspension Triggers']
  ExportWithMetadata          capability_answer            [not null, default: 'Unknown', note: 'If the records had to leave, would their metadata and audit history go with them? Export that drops the metadata is the second lawful shape failing. Bare files are not records']
  OriginalDateIsPreserved     capability_answer            [not null, default: 'Unknown', note: 'Can the date the ORIGINAL record was created be stored separately from the date it was scanned or loaded? Most line-of-business systems stamp the capture date and have nowhere to put the other one']

  // --- Evidence and method -------------------------------------------------
  // The three multi-value columns. Each of them qualifies one of the answers
  // above, and each is a list because the honest answer is a list.
  SuspensionTriggers          disposal_suspension_trigger[] [note: 'Which grounds for freezing disposal this platform can actually honour. Tick every one it does. None ticked, with Disposal Can Be Suspended set to Yes, is the contradiction to chase']
  AuditEventsLogged           audit_event[]                [note: 'Which events reach the audit log and stay there. Export and Delete are the two that decide whether a disposal action can ever be evidenced']
  ExportMethods               export_method[]              [note: 'Every route the records could leave by. Leave it empty if there is genuinely none - an empty set is a finding, and a view is built on it']

  // --- Verdict and follow-up -----------------------------------------------
  DestinationVerdict          destination_verdict          [not null, default: 'Not assessed', note: 'The assessor answer, typed by a person. Nothing computes it: recording that the answers say no and the risk is accepted with named configuration is the whole job']
  VerdictBasis                longtext                     [note: 'Why the verdict is what it is, in a few sentences: what was demonstrated, what was taken on trust, and what has to be configured. Categories of record only, never examples, and no patient or client identifiers']
  FollowUpRequired            boolean                      [not null, default: false, note: 'Whether anything has to happen before this verdict can be relied on. The one Yes/No column here, because the assessor is the person who would know']
  FollowUpAction              nvarchar                     [note: 'The single next action, in one line, with who owns it. If it needs a paragraph it needs a project, and the project belongs somewhere else']

  // --- Assessment provenance -----------------------------------------------
  // Without these the register is hearsay: a verdict with no author and no
  // date cannot be re-opened, aged, or defended two years later.
  AssessedBy                  person                       [note: 'Who made the call. Not the custodian who filled in the answers - the person who signed off the verdict']
  AssessmentDate              date                         [note: 'The date the verdict was reached. A verdict older than the platform version it was made against is a verdict to redo']

  // Exactly the columns a declared view filters on, and nothing else. Four of
  // twenty slots. SharePoint cannot index a multi-value column at all
  // (measured, with a control that stuck on a single-value Choice), so the one
  // view that filters Export Methods is paired with Lifecycle Status, which
  // serves the query on its own.
  indexes {
    LifecycleStatus
    DestinationVerdict
    BusinessDomain
    FollowUpRequired
  }

  Note: 'One row per business platform. Whether a digitised record can be kept there: retention, disposal, suspension, audit and export, with a verdict.'
}
